// Practice quiz: CREATE TABLE, data types and constraints
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/relational-design/create-table-data-types-and-constraints

::o1 money type::Which type stores a price such as 19.99 exactly, with two decimal places? {
=numeric(8,2) #Correct. numeric stores exact decimals; PostgreSQL recommends it for money.
~integer #Not quite. integer stores whole numbers only.
~boolean #Not quite. boolean stores true or false.
~date #Not quite. date stores a calendar day.
}

::o1 date or timestamp::A column must record the exact moment an order was placed, including the time of day. Which type fits? {
=timestamp #Correct. timestamp holds a date plus a time of day.
~date #Not quite. date holds only the day, so the time would be lost.
~text #Not quite. text would accept anything, including values that are not moments.
~integer #Not quite. integer holds whole numbers, not dates and times.
}

::o1 varchar limit::A column is declared name varchar(5). What happens when you insert 'Alexandra'? {
=It is rejected with "value too long for type character varying(5)" #Correct. varchar(n) accepts at most n characters; a longer value is an error.
~It is stored as 'Alexa' #Not quite. PostgreSQL does not cut the value short on insert; it raises an error.
~It is stored in full #Not quite. The limit is enforced.
~It is stored as null #Not quite. A value that does not fit is an error, not null.
}

::o2 choose a constraint::Rule: "No two users may have the same username." Which constraint enforces it? {
=UNIQUE on username #Correct. UNIQUE rejects a second row with the same value.
~NOT NULL on username #Not quite. NOT NULL requires a value but allows repeats.
~DEFAULT on username #Not quite. DEFAULT only fills in a value when the insert leaves the column out.
~CHECK (username \= username) #Not quite. That condition is always true for a non-null value, so it rejects nothing.
}

::o2 default value::tasks has done boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false. You run INSERT INTO tasks (id, title) VALUES (1, 'Read'); What is stored in done? {
=false #Correct. The insert leaves done out, so its DEFAULT, false, is used.
~null #Not quite. Null would be the default only if no DEFAULT were declared; NOT NULL would then reject it.
~true #Not quite. The declared default is false.
~The insert is rejected because done is missing #Not quite. A column with a DEFAULT can be left out.
}

::o3 name the rule::An insert fails with: new row for relation "products" violates check constraint "products_price_check". Which rule failed? {
=A CHECK condition on price #Correct. The message says check constraint, and the name points at the price column.
~The primary key of products #Not quite. A primary key error says duplicate key value and names products_pkey.
~A foreign key from products #Not quite. A foreign key error says violates foreign key constraint.
~NOT NULL on price #Not quite. A not-null error says null value in column ... violates not-null constraint.
}

::o3 predict the rejection::members has email text NOT NULL UNIQUE, and a member with email kai@example.com exists. Which insert is rejected? {
=INSERT INTO members (id, email) VALUES (9, 'kai@example.com'); #Correct. The email repeats, so the UNIQUE constraint rejects the row.
~INSERT INTO members (id, email) VALUES (9, 'lee@example.com'); #Not quite. That email is new and present.
~INSERT INTO members (id, email) VALUES (10, 'KAI2@example.com'); #Not quite. A different value passes UNIQUE.
~None of these #Not quite. A repeated email breaks the UNIQUE rule.
}
